---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="How DNS Helps My Portfolio Load | Anas Mansour"
  description="A walkthrough of how DNS resolves anasabdelmotelb.github.io: the resolver, nameservers, DNS records, the response, TTL and CNAME records."
>
  <article class="note container">
    <p class="back"><a href="/#writing">← Back to Writing &amp; Technical Notes</a></p>
    <p class="eyebrow">Technical note</p>
    <h1>How DNS Helps My Portfolio Load</h1>

    <section aria-labelledby="dns-what">
      <h2 id="dns-what">1. What is DNS?</h2>
      <p>
        DNS stands for Domain Name System. I think of it as an address book for the internet. When I type
        <code>anasabdelmotelb.github.io</code> into my browser, my computer needs to find the correct address
        for the website. DNS helps it find that address.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="dns-resolver">
      <h2 id="dns-resolver">2. What does the Resolver do?</h2>
      <p>
        The recursive resolver looks for the DNS information needed to reach the website. First, it checks if
        the answer is already saved in its cache. If the answer is not available or has expired, it looks for
        the information by asking other DNS servers.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="dns-nameserver">
      <h2 id="dns-nameserver">3. What is a Nameserver?</h2>
      <p>
        DNS has different levels of nameservers. The resolver can start with a root nameserver, which directs
        it to the correct top-level domain server, such as <code>.io</code>. The <code>.io</code> nameserver
        then helps it find the authoritative nameserver for the domain. The authoritative nameserver provides
        the DNS records for the requested hostname.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="dns-record">
      <h2 id="dns-record">4. What is a DNS Record and Response?</h2>
      <p>
        A DNS record stores information about a domain. For example, an A record connects a hostname to an
        IPv4 address. My portfolio is hosted on GitHub Pages, and its DNS lookup returns GitHub Pages IP
        addresses, including <code>185.199.108.153</code>, <code>185.199.109.153</code>,
        <code>185.199.110.153</code>, and <code>185.199.111.153</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        After the resolver gets the answer, it returns the result to my computer. The browser can then connect
        to the website using HTTPS. DNS records also have a TTL (Time to Live), which controls how long the
        answer can stay in the cache before it needs to be checked again.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="dns-cname">
      <h2 id="dns-cname">5. What is a CNAME?</h2>
      <p>
        A CNAME record points one hostname to another hostname instead of directly storing an IP address. For
        example, if I use a custom subdomain such as <code>portfolio.example.com</code> in the future, I could
        configure a CNAME record pointing it to <code>anasabdelmotelb.github.io</code>, following GitHub Pages'
        configuration requirements.
      </p>
      <p>
        I currently use my GitHub Pages address, so this CNAME example explains a possible future setup, not a
        custom domain I have already configured.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="dns-conclusion">
      <h2 id="dns-conclusion">Conclusion</h2>
      <p>
        DNS is important because it helps my browser find the server for a website by using its domain name.
        The resolver finds the answer, nameservers help provide the correct records, and the browser uses the
        returned address to connect to the website. Caching and TTL help make future lookups more efficient.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

